Q: How to convert the input to octal and unicode in c A: <p>You are misundertanding what is a <em>value</em> and what is an <em>encoding</em>. For example the character 'A' (assuming ascii, Latin or unicode) has a decimal value of 65, an octal value of 0o101 or an hexadecimal one of 0x41.</p>
<p>Said differently, the following statements are perfectly equivalent:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>char c = 'A';  // Assuming ASCII, Latin or UTF-8
char c = 65;   // decimal
char c = 0101; // octal
char c = 0x41; // hexadecimal
</code></pre>
<p>When you write <code>input_number == 53</code> you are comparing to the <em>decimal</em> 53 which is the ASCII representation of <code>'5'</code>. You should at least write <code>input_number == 053</code> to compare to the octal <code>053</code> which is indeed the decimal <code>43</code> representing the ASCII character <code>'+'</code>.</p>
<p>But there is worse: your functions consistently return pointers which point to character strings (which are mere character arrays) <em>declared inside the function</em>. While you return a pointer to a literal string, everything will be fine. But if you try to compute the value, you will get a <em>dangling</em> pointer:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>char *convert_to_octal_string(int input_number)
{
    char str[2] = {0};
    str[0] = input_number; 
    // fine till here: if input_number is 053, str contains &quot;+&quot;
    return str;  // BOOM dangling pointer
}
</code></pre>
<p>The problem is that the lifetime of the <code>str</code> ends at the return statement and using it afterwards invokes Undefined Behaviour (the hell for C programmers). In common implementations, it means the the memory of <code>str</code> will be reused and will contain <em>anything</em> when you will use it.</p>
<p>You should try and use good tutorials because your current code is totally broken.</p>

